## Turn Vague Process Notes Into Steps You Can Repeat

If your process notes lean on words like "momentum," "seamless," "robust," or "transformative," stop and replace them with actions your team can actually follow.

Those words feel useful when you write them. They fail when you come back a week later and need to ship.

{{< compare >}}
{{< before >}}
- Keep momentum between planning and implementation.
- Create a seamless handoff from design to code.
- Build a robust review loop.
- Use a transformative feedback cycle.
{{< /before >}}
{{< after >}}
- End planning with one written decision, one owner, and one next task.
- Put design notes, edge cases, and copy in the ticket before coding starts.
- Require one reviewer to verify behavior, tests, and rollback risk.
- Turn review feedback into code changes or follow-up issues before merging.
{{< /after >}}
{{< /compare >}}

The second version gives you something to check. You can see who owns the work, what has to exist before the next step starts, and what "done" means at each handoff.

Write your process notes so a tired teammate can still use them. That usually means verbs, files, owners, and checks. If a sentence cannot tell someone what to do next, cut it or rewrite it.

You also need each step to pull the reader forward. Clear actions help, but they are not enough if the order stays fuzzy. The next section should show how to sequence those steps so work does not stall between review and merge.

# Entity Access Policies

[Waaseyaa](./) lets you enforce read, create, update, and delete rules in code. You define policy classes per entity type and keep authorization logic close to the data model.

## Install The Access Package

```bash
composer require waaseyaa/entity-access
```

This installs the access policy package for your project. Composer adds the package and resolves its dependencies.

## Register A Policy

Attach a policy to your entity type so the framework knows which rules to evaluate.

```php
use Waaseyaa\Entity\Attributes\EntityType;
use App\Access\ArticlePolicy;

#[EntityType(
    id: 'article',
    label: 'Article',
    policy: ArticlePolicy::class,
)]
final class Article
{
}
```

This binds `ArticlePolicy` to the `article` entity type. Every entity operation for articles now flows through that policy.

## Write Your Access Rules

Define each operation as a method on the policy class. Return `true` to allow the operation. Return `false` to deny it.

```php
namespace App\Access;

use App\Entities\Article;
use App\Models\User;

final class ArticlePolicy
{
    public function view(User $user, Article $article): bool
    {
        return $article->isPublished() || $user->id === $article->authorId();
    }

    public function update(User $user, Article $article): bool
    {
        return $user->id === $article->authorId();
    }

    public function delete(User $user, Article $article): bool
    {
        return $user->hasRole('admin');
    }
}
```

This policy allows public reads for published articles. It limits edits to the author and deletes to admins.

## Check Access In Application Code

Call the access service before you perform sensitive work. That keeps controllers, actions, and handlers explicit.

```php
if (! $access->allows('update', $article, $currentUser)) {
    throw new ForbiddenException();
}

$article->rename($input['title']);
$articles->save($article);
```

This checks the `update` rule for the current article and user. If access fails, execution stops before the write happens.

## Gate Field-Level Changes

You can protect sensitive fields with the same pattern. Use field checks when an entity is editable but a specific field needs tighter control.

```php
if (! $access->allowsField('status', 'update', $article, $currentUser)) {
    unset($input['status']);
}
```

This prevents unauthorized users from changing the `status` field. The rest of the update can still proceed.

## Test Your Policies

Write direct policy tests. Keep them small and focused on the rule you care about.

```php
public function test_author_can_update_own_article(): void
{
    $policy = new ArticlePolicy();
    $user = User::factory()->make(['id' => 10]);
    $article = Article::factory()->make(['author_id' => 10]);

    $this->assertTrue($policy->update($user, $article));
}
```

This test checks one rule with one clear expectation. Small policy tests make authorization regressions easy to catch.

## Keep Policies Predictable

Put business rules in the policy. Keep transport concerns, redirects, and response formatting outside it.

Use one policy per entity type. If a rule depends on roles, ownership, or publication state, express that directly in code.
